"enc-name">Han Chinese</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Han Chinese are the majority ethnic group of China, taking their name from the Han Dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/han-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Han Dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Han Dynasty ruled China from 202 BCE to 220 CE, with a brief interruption, and is regarded as a formative era of Chinese civilisation.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-fei"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/han-fei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Han Fei</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Han Fei (c. 280–233 BCE) was a prince of the State of Han and the leading theorist of Legalism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-guang"><span class="enc-name">Han Guang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Han Guang was a commander sent north by Wu Chen, the rebel king of Zhao, during the revolt against the Qin, who instead made himself King of Yan in 209 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-liner"><span class="enc-name">Han Lin'er</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Han Lin'er was the son of the White Lotus leader Han Shantong, who launched the Red Turban revolt against the Yuan dynasty in 1351.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-river"><span class="enc-name">Han River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Han River is a major tributary of the Yangtze in central China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-shantong"><span class="enc-name">Han Shantong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Han Shantong was a Chinese leader of the White Lotus sect from Hebei who preached the imminent coming of the Buddha Maitreya and claimed descent from the Song emperors.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-shizhong"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/han-shizhong.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Han Shizhong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Han Shizhong was a general of the Song dynasty and one of the leading commanders who defended the Southern Song against the Jurchen Jin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-system"><span class="enc-name">Han system</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The han system was the organisation of Tokugawa Japan into domains, called han, each governed by a daimyo who owed allegiance to the shogun.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-tuozhou"><span class="enc-name">Han Tuozhou</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Han Tuozhou was a chief minister of the Southern Song dynasty, related by marriage to the imperial family, who dominated the court from the 1190s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-xin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/han-xin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Han Xin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Han Xin was the leading general of Liu Bang in the Chu–Han war that founded the Han dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/han-zhao"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/han-zhao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Han Zhao</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">Han Zhao was a state of northern China founded in 304 by Liu Yuan, a Xiongnu leader, at the start of the Sixteen Kingdoms period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school"><span class="enc-name">Hanafi school</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Hanafi school is one of the four main schools of Sunni Islamic law, named after the eighth-century jurist Abu Hanifa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hanamkonda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hanamkonda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hanamkonda</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hanamkonda is a town in Telangana, India, now part of the Warangal urban area.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/hancheng"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/hancheng.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Hancheng</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Hancheng is a county-level city in eastern Shaanxi, on the west bank of the Yellow River opposite Shanxi.</span></a></li></ul>
